CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ Example 4: #3: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ non-pack is more specialized: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ only the pack matches: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.deduct.type]/9.2, [temp.spec.partial.order]: during partial
ordering, if A_i was originally a pack expansion and P_i is not, deduction
fails, so R<int, T> is more specialized than R<int, T...>.
partial_specialization_at_least_as_specialised deduced T from `T...', both
specializations were at least as specialized as the other, and the sorted
keys picked the pack (bin53).  libstdc++ 13's views::__adaptor::_Partial has
this shape (the pack and the single-argument constrained specializations).
S<int, const int &, const int &> (Example 4, #2, with two elements) still
selects the primary template.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program,
and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
